module novac.check

// Donor: none -- a file of message TEXTS has no design to borrow; the texts are this
//        language's own diagnostics, and their wording rules live in the conventions.
// Role: check layer, DATA -- the refusal texts the checker hands to the user.
// Used by: rules.nv and the other check/ files, by bare name (a folder is one module).
// Guarded by: check-novac-subset-debt-dated.py (a refusal owes a stage, counted two
//        ways), check-novac-file-size.py (why these texts live apart at all).

/// THE TEXTS of the checker's refusals -- what the user actually reads.
///
/// They live apart from `rules.nv` because they are a different subject with a
/// different reason to change. A text changes when a wording is rewritten, a stage is
/// quoted, or a D-block is cited; a rule in `rules.nv` changes when the language does.
/// Holding both in one file is what pushed it past the 1000-line limit (274, decision
/// 12), and the limit was right: the texts grow faster than the rules do.
///
/// A folder is ONE module in Nova, so this is the same module as `rules.nv`: nothing
/// here is exported, nothing there imports it, and every name stays visible exactly as
/// it was before the split.
///
/// TWO MEASURES READ THESE TEXTS, which is itself a reason to keep them together:
/// `check-novac-subset-debt-dated` counts the refusals that owe a stage, and its
/// by-door half asks which door each text goes through. A text is invisible to the
/// first and visible to the second when its wording is not canonical -- and the
/// disagreement between the two is how three blind spots were found in one evening.

/// One text for the position rule.
const QUALIFIER_POSITION_MSG = "this qualifier is not legal here (`ro` is the default and redundant on a parameter; type-position `mut` is retracted there and redundant on a result)"

/// A declared type whose SHAPE the subset does not compile yet -- a slice, a
/// tuple, a function type, a qualified type. The parser reads them all (plan
/// 9.4: the grammar is the language's, the subset is the checker's), so the
/// refusal names the shape instead of pretending the type is missing.
/// The largest `int` literal, as TEXT. `int` is intptr_t (Nova's rule, like
/// Go's intgo) and 64 bits on every target novac emits for; the bound lives
/// here as digits because the check compares digits -- parsing the literal
/// into the type whose range is in question would inherit the very defect
/// this rule exists to catch (#810).
const INT_MAX_TEXT = "9223372036854775807"

/// The largest MAGNITUDE any integer row novac knows can ever hold -- `u64`,
/// the widest (registry #1250). The eager Lit-walk door (`int_text_fits`)
/// used `INT_MAX_TEXT` here until #1250: that bound is `int`'s own, one row
/// among several, and comparing every literal against it before the literal's
/// OWN position decided its row refused `take(18446744073709551615)` at a
/// `u64` parameter -- legal Nova the row door (`int_text_fits_row`) accepts
/// once it is asked. `U64_MAX_TEXT` is the true ceiling: nothing wider fits
/// any row this language has, signed or not.
const U64_MAX_TEXT = "18446744073709551615"

const INT_TOO_LARGE_MSG = "this integer literal does not fit any integer type novac knows -- the language makes that a hard compile error (E_LIT_OUT_OF_RANGE, 03-syntax.md), not a silent truncation; the oracle wraps it in the emitted C (#810)"

/// The nesting refusal (#807/#808). The number is the oracle's 512, which is
/// normative there; the whole corpus never writes past 40 levels.
const NESTING_TOO_DEEP_MSG = "outside the subset: nesting is deeper than 512 levels -- the parser stops before the stack does (#807)"

const DUP_DECL_MSG = "this top-level name is declared twice in one file -- one namespace, so the second declaration collides (E_DUP_DEFINITION, D29); the oracle drops the second body in silence (#811)"

/// A primitive is a CLOSED type (D35: "no `type str {...}` over the built-in
/// `str` -- only methods"). This is the NORM's refusal, not a subset stage:
/// it never lifts.
/// The nested-interpolation judge's answer (I2): the lexer splits one
/// level; a literal with a live slot inside another slot stays whole, and
/// the chain has no arm for it yet.
const NESTED_INTERP_MSG = "outside the subset: an interpolation nested inside another slot is not compiled yet (E2-b3)"

/// The bare-self judge's answer: `@` is a value only where a method body
/// bound its receiver.
const BARE_SELF_MSG = "`@` is the method receiver -- outside a method body there is nothing it could name"

/// The receiver-field judge's answer (#1459): `@field` -- written alone or as
/// the receiver punning `T { @field }` (D52 rule 1) -- reads the receiver, and
/// only a method body binds one.
const SELF_FIELD_OUTSIDE_MSG = "`@field` reads a field of the method receiver -- outside a method body there is no receiver to read it from"

/// The slot-type judge's answer for a NON-PRIMITIVE slot: the spec routes the
/// slot through `Display` -- `@display(mut f Fmt)` (D422), with `#impl(Display)`
/// the opt-in gate that a bare `${x}` needs (D186 amendment) -- and that is
/// protocol dispatch, wave B1b of 274.7. Every primitive family is compiled
/// since B1a (2026-09-05): before it the slot was held to `{str, int}` by id.
///
/// CORRECTED 2026-09-18: this doc and the message below said `Into[str]` and
/// cited D73 for it. D73 RETRACTED `From`/`Into` on 2026-07-06 (08-runtime.md),
/// so the refusal sent the reader to a protocol the language does not have --
/// and wrote the method as bare `display(...)`, the spelling D209 retracted.
/// A diagnostic naming a form that does not exist is worse than a vague one:
/// the reader goes looking, and the search succeeds only in the old spec.
/// `#impl` IS WRITTEN ONCE (D186, `02-types.md:8887`: several protocols go in a
/// single annotation separated by `+`, two annotations are not allowed). A
/// LANGUAGE error, not subset debt: the form does not exist, as opposed to "not
/// compiled yet" -- hence the `@reject_*` door rather than `@report_*`.
///
/// Written 2026-09-18 in the same step that taught the checker to READ `#impl`.
/// While the attribute was refused wholesale the doubled form never got through
/// -- BY ACCIDENT -- and the first edit that read it let the form in. The list
/// of forms was asked of the `spec-reader` agent, and this one was missing from
/// the grid the edit had been probed against.
/// `#impl` CARRIES AT LEAST ONE PROTOCOL (D186 amendment 2026-09-18). Both
/// `#impl()` and a bare `#impl` are LANGUAGE errors, not subset debt: the
/// annotation exists to say the author opted IN, and opting into nothing opens
/// no gate and asserts nothing, while still reading to a human as a decision.
///
/// ONE message for both spellings on purpose: the difference between `#impl()`
/// and `#impl` is in the writing, not in what the author said, and two codes for
/// one mistake would send the reader looking for a distinction that is not there.
const IMPL_NO_PROTOCOLS_MSG = "`#impl` names at least one protocol: `#impl(Display)` or `#impl(A + B)` -- an empty annotation opts into nothing (D186)"

const IMPL_REPEATED_MSG = "`#impl` is written once: several protocols go in ONE annotation, separated by `+` -- `#impl(A + B)`, not two lines (D186)"

const INTERP_TYPE_MSG = "outside the subset: an interpolation slot of a non-primitive type without its own `@display(mut f Fmt)` (`Option[T]`, a record that relies on `#impl(Display)`, a generic instance) is not compiled yet (274.7 B1b) -- every primitive family and every plain type with `@display` is"

/// The one primitive the runtime cannot display: `std.runtime.string_builder`
/// has no unsigned 64-bit `display_spec`, and the oracle prints `u64`/`uint`
/// through the int one, truncating -- the oracle's deviation from the spec,
/// not a rule. novac refuses by name until std gains the function (274.7 B1,
/// open question to the owner), rather than truncate in silence.
const INTERP_U64_MSG = "outside the subset: an unsigned 64-bit interpolation slot is not compiled yet (274.7 B1c) -- the runtime has no uint_display_spec, and truncating through the int one is what the spec forbids"

/// The constant-initializer boundary of I1: substitution happens at the use
/// site, and the hoist never ran there.
/// D184's strict module-level partition, both directions over ONE predicate
/// (`sem/constexpr.nv`): a constexpr initializer is written `const`, a
/// runtime one `ro`. LANGUAGE errors, the oracle's codes named in the text.
const RO_FOR_CONSTEXPR_MSG = "a module-level `ro` whose initializer is known at compile time is written `const` (D184, E_RO_FOR_CONSTEXPR_PREFER_CONST) -- `ro` is for a value that needs the runtime: a call, an allocation"
const CONST_NOT_CONSTEXPR_MSG = "a `const` initializer is known at compile time -- literals, operators over constants, record literals and sum constructors of them (D184, E_CONST_NOT_CONSTEXPR); a value that needs the runtime -- a call, an allocation such as an array literal -- is written `ro`"

/// Module values whose initializers read each other in a CYCLE (274.5 S6):
/// the D184 amendment of 2026-09-30 (`03-syntax.md:8137`) initializes them in
/// dependency order, and a cycle has none -- E_MODULE_INIT_CYCLE, a language
/// error, with the path in the text as the oracle prints it.
fn module_init_cycle_msg(path str) -> str =>
    "module-level `ro` initializers form a cycle: ${path} -- no initialization order computes them (D184 amendment 2026-09-30, E_MODULE_INIT_CYCLE)"

const INTERP_CONST_MSG = "outside the subset: a constant whose initializer interpolates is not compiled yet (E2-b3)"


/// The refutable-binding judge's answer (hunt var_shadow, #875): a binding
/// whose NAME is a visible payload-less variant is a one-variant pattern
/// match in the language (D411), and the oracle refuses it -- novac's own
/// refusal is `@reject_first_leaf_of(kids, REFUTABLE_BIND_MSG)` in
/// check/binds.nv; it used to accept and shadow the variant in silence.
const REFUTABLE_BIND_MSG = "a binding pattern that names a variant is refutable -- rename the binding, or match on the value (D411)"

/// The interpolated-message judge's answer (hunt N5, #873): the message is
/// spelled into C as ONE literal, so `${...}` inside it would print itself
/// verbatim on violation -- a silent lie about the values. Refused until the
/// interpolation wave computes it.
const INTERP_CONTRACT_MSG_MSG = "outside the subset: an interpolated contract message is not compiled yet (E2) -- spell the message without `\${...}`"

/// The argument-form judge's answer (hunt N2, #870): a form the expression
/// walk does not know used to FALL OUT of both walks silently -- the
/// argument was neither refused nor printed. A branch is never punctuation.
const PRINTLN_ARG_FORM_MSG = "outside the subset: this form is not compiled in an argument position yet (E2-b) -- bind it to a name first"

/// The variant-value judge's answer (the variant-value wave): a variant
/// that CARRIES a payload is a constructor, not a value -- name it with its
/// arguments. A payload-less one is a value and compiles to its maker.
const VARIANT_VALUE_NEEDS_ARGS_MSG = "outside the subset: this variant carries a payload -- construct it with arguments, `Name(...)`"

/// The if-let judges' two answers (if-let wave). The subset takes exactly
/// the shape its carriers write: `Some` and an Option-instance initializer.
/// A `match` whose scrutinee is an APPLIED sum -- `Option[int]`, `Result[T, E]`.
///
/// The form is recognised now (the arm's head is found through the
/// DECLARATION, so `Some` is no longer called a variant of another sum), and
/// it is refused HERE because the emitter cannot spell it: an applied sum
/// reaching novac comes from the SHELL, with the oracle's tag names
/// (`NOVA_TAG_Option_Some`) and value semantics, while novac writes its own
/// spelling and a pointer access. Measured 2026-09-20: `_novac_l2->tag` on a
/// value struct and an undeclared `NOVAC_TAG_..._Option_Some`.
///
/// A user's own generic sum never reaches this door -- generic parameters are
/// refused earlier (E2-b2) -- so this refusal is about the shell's sums alone.
///
/// NOT a constant (registry #1233): the applied sum's own name is part of
/// the message (`ty_source_name(@ctx, scr_t)`), built at the one call site
/// in `match_arms.nv` that already has `@ctx` -- a static string here named
/// `Option[int]` regardless of which applied sum actually fired the gate.

/// A LITERAL pattern against a value of another type (the string family,
/// E2-b): `'a'` over a `str`, `"a"` over a `char` or an `int`, `3` over a
/// `str`. D128 keeps `char` apart from `int`, and the spec's pattern table
/// says a literal compares "by value" (syntax.ru.md, pattern kinds) -- a value
/// of ANOTHER type is no comparison at all. The oracle has no check of its
/// own here: it prints `scr.len == ...` over an int and clang refuses the C.
const LIT_PATTERN_TYPE_MSG = "a literal pattern compares with a value of its own type -- a string literal with a `str`, a char literal with a `char` (D128: `char` is not `int`); the value matched here is of another type"

/// A STRING or CHAR literal standing INSIDE a `match` arm's pattern --
/// `W("x")`, `(c, 'a')`. Legal Nova (D486 s2), and refused BY NAME because the
/// lowering tests the arm's ROOT only: for an int the tag of `A(3)` is tested
/// and the `3` dropped, so `A(4)` takes the arm (measured on the day the
/// string arms opened, 2026-10-01). These two kinds were refused at every
/// depth until then; opening the root without this door would have handed
/// them the same silent wrong answer.
const NESTED_LITERAL_ARM_MSG = "outside the subset: a string or char literal inside a `match` arm's pattern is read but not compiled yet -- the lowering tests the arm's root only (E2-b, with nested pattern tests)"

const IFLET_VARIANT_MSG = "outside the subset: `if <pattern> = ...` takes only `Some(name)` here -- other variants arrive with their carriers (274.5 P4)"

/// A tail `if let` used as a VALUE with no `else` (registry 1189).
///
/// Not a subset limit but a fact about the shape: the None path produces
/// nothing, so the function would fall off its end. The plain tail `if` is
/// refused in the same words for the same reason (274.7 B14).
const IFLET_VALUE_NEEDS_ELSE_MSG = "outside the subset: a tail `if let` needs an `else` to be a value -- without it the None path produces nothing (E2-b)"

/// The two branches of a value `if let` disagreed on a type.
///
/// The agreement is the whole rule, and the cost of skipping it is silent:
/// the emitter declares ONE temporary and writes both branches into it, so a
/// compiler that took the first branch's type would produce valid C with a
/// wrong answer and no diagnostic anywhere.
const IFLET_VALUE_DISAGREE_MSG = "outside the subset: the branches of a value `if let` must agree on a type (E2-b)"

/// See IFLET_VARIANT_MSG above; the initializer half.
const IFLET_INIT_MSG = "the initializer of `if Some(...) = ...` must be an Option instance -- this expression's type is not one"

/// The if-let condition's PATTERN door (wave P3, D486 §4's third column):
/// the condition takes a variant pattern -- judged against the initializer's
/// sum and lowered through the same doors as a match arm. Every other shape
/// is refused BY NAME rather than fallen through: a NAME binding (bare or
/// with a mode word -- the oracle's E_AMBIGUOUS_IDENT_PATTERN /
/// E_CONSUME_IN_CONDITION territory) is read but not lowered yet, and a
/// tuple/record/array/literal/`_` condition the lowering has no test for.
const IFLET_COND_BIND_MSG = "outside the subset: a name binding in an `if` condition is read but not compiled yet -- the condition takes a variant pattern (274.5 P3, the condition-forms wave)"
const IFLET_COND_FORM_MSG = "outside the subset: an `if` condition takes a variant pattern here -- tuple, record and array conditions arrive with the condition-forms wave (274.5 P3)"

/// The `ro` word on a pattern binder OUTSIDE an if/while condition (wave P3):
/// the word is read everywhere so the condition can take it (the oracle's
/// E_AMBIGUOUS_IDENT_PATTERN names the bare form there), but a match arm or a
/// destructuring binding has no use for it -- the oracle refuses the arm form
/// (`expected pattern, got ro`, measured 2026-09-29), and a refusal by name
/// beats a silent mis-read as `consume` (the two mode doors map words, and an
/// unlisted word used to fall into the `else` arm).
const RO_WORD_PATTERN_MSG = "`ro` stands only before a bare name that is the whole condition pattern (D486): a binder inside a pattern is read-only without the word -- `Some(x)`, not `Some(ro x)`"

/// The while-let judges (wave P4): the SAME third column of D486 s4 as the
/// if-let's, worded for its own position -- a while-let is the if-let's
/// condition asked once per iteration, so the judgments are one and the
/// messages name the form the author wrote.
const WHILELET_VARIANT_MSG = "outside the subset: `while <pattern> = ...` takes only `Some(name)` here -- other variants arrive with their carriers (274.5 P4)"
const WHILELET_INIT_MSG = "the initializer of `while Some(...) = ...` must be an Option instance -- this expression's type is not one"
const WHILELET_COND_BIND_MSG = "outside the subset: a name binding in a `while` condition is read but not compiled yet -- the condition takes a variant pattern (274.5 P4, the condition-forms wave)"
const WHILELET_COND_FORM_MSG = "outside the subset: a `while` condition takes a variant pattern here -- record, array and positional-group conditions arrive with the condition-forms wave (274.5 P4)"

/// The for-CALL head judge's answer (wave B16): the head must hand back a
/// vector -- an instance of arity one -- or the loop has no element type.
///
/// NAMED AS DEBT AND DATED 2026-09-13, because it was neither. The refusal always spoke
/// about ITSELF, which is why it never looked wrong -- but D58 (amended by plan 138)
/// leaves no choice: `for x in c` desugars through `c.iter()` and `next()`, ALWAYS. So
/// requiring `[]T` is a LAG, not a rule, and a reader is entitled to know when it ends.
/// Carrying neither spelling, it was also invisible to the debt ratchet -- the hole in
/// that counter's scope is wave B11's subject.
const FOR_CALL_HEAD_MSG = "outside the subset: the head of this `for` is a call that does not return a vector, and novac walks a collection by index, so it needs `[]T` -- the iterator protocol D58 asks for is not compiled yet (274.7 B12)"

/// The for-header's OWN predicate (wave P5, D486 s4: the header is a binding
/// position, and a binding position is irrefutable): a variant, a literal, an
/// array or a disjunction can fail to match, and the loop has no `else` for
/// the failure. The language's own code, as in REFUTABLE_BINDING_MSG.
const FOR_REFUTABLE_MSG = "a `for` header takes an irrefutable pattern -- this form can fail to match, and the loop has no `else` (D486 s4, E_REFUTABLE_BINDING)"

/// B15 bounds: a tuple parameter's call-site ABI is unmeasured and novac
/// B15 bounds: a tuple parameter's call-site ABI is unmeasured and novac
/// itself carries none -- the boundary is a judge, not unreachability.
const TUPLE_PARAM_MSG = "outside the subset: a tuple as a PARAMETER is not compiled yet (B15 bounds) -- return one, or pass the components"

const PRIM_REDECL_MSG = "a primitive is a closed type (D35): its name cannot be redeclared -- add methods to it instead"

/// D125/D371 let a user declaration SHADOW a prelude/handed-over name (user
/// wins, a warning at most) -- but novac's C name for its own types does not
/// carry the module yet (D381), so a shadowing type would print a second
/// `struct Nova_<name>` beside the shell's and clang refuses the unit
/// (measured 2026-09-01: ct.c carried both). Refused BY NAME until the
/// module-qualified C names arrive; the norm itself stays wider on purpose.
const SHADOW_FOREIGN_MSG = "outside the subset: the interop shell already declares a struct under this name's C spelling -- a second definition in the unit is refused by clang; pick another name (goes the day novac emits the whole prelude)"

/// The three ways a record construction can disagree with its declaration
/// (#812). The spec states the first two as numbered rules
/// (`02-types.md:1677`): every declared field must be present, and a name the
/// type does not have is an error. The third is the same rule read once more:
/// naming one field twice fills the count without filling the record.
const CTOR_MISSING_FIELD_MSG = "this record construction omits a declared field -- construction always requires every field (D17, spec 02-types.md:152 and :1677) (#812)"

const CTOR_UNKNOWN_FIELD_MSG = "this record construction names a field the type does not declare (#812)"

const CTOR_FIELD_TWICE_MSG = "this record construction names a field twice, so another declared field is missing (#812)"

/// `ro x ro T` -- the oracle's E_REDUNDANT_TYPE_MODIFIER (registry 1458).
const REDUNDANT_TYPE_RO_MSG = "`ro` after a `ro` binding is redundant -- the `ro` binding already makes the content read-only (D33/D176 amendment, Plan 124.8, E_REDUNDANT_TYPE_MODIFIER); write `ro x T` instead of `ro x ro T`"

const TYPE_FORM_MSG = "outside the subset: this type form is not compiled yet (slices, tuples, fn types and qualifiers arrive with generics, E2-b)"


/// `if c { a } else { b }` standing where a VALUE is expected. The STATEMENT
/// form is compiled -- including its `else` since 2026-08-18 -- but as a value
/// it needs a temporary the emitter does not build yet, exactly like `match`
/// outside an initializer.
const IF_EXPR_MSG = "outside the subset: an `if` in value position is not compiled yet"

/// `(a, b)` -- a tuple value. Read under its own kind since wave B15 (the
/// comma is what tells it from grouping parens); compiling it is steps 3-4
/// of that wave -- the pair term in the interner, then the D123 mono struct.

/// `@field` -- the receiver's own field. It can only appear inside a method
/// body, and novac does not declare methods yet, so this refusal and the
/// method-declaration one are two doors of the same limit.
const SELF_FIELD_MSG = "outside the subset: `@field` arrives with methods"

/// `ro x int = 1` -- a local with a DECLARED type. The subset infers the type
/// from the initializer; honouring a written one means checking the two agree,
/// and that is the inference stage's work (E2-b3).
const TYPED_LOCAL_MSG = "outside the subset: a declared local type is not compiled yet (E2-b3)"

/// The same limit, said WITH THE NAME the reader came for.
///
/// Two facts, and the order between them is the whole point. The subset's limit
/// is what refuses the program -- that stays first, because it is the truth
/// about why nothing compiled. But a name nobody declared is what the reader
/// can FIX, and the old text mentioned neither, so a misspelling in
/// `ro x Pointt = 1` read as "novac cannot do declared local types yet" and the
/// typo stayed invisible. Registry #1150: of seven positions where a type name
/// can be written, this was the one that blamed the subset for a user's error.
fn typed_local_msg(name str, declared bool) -> str {
    if declared {
        return "${TYPED_LOCAL_MSG} -- the type written here is `${name}`"
    } else {
        // Nobody declares it: two problems, and only one of them is the
        // reader's. Both are named, his first.
    }
    "${TYPED_LOCAL_MSG}; and no type named `${name}` is declared in this file or in the declarations handed to novac -- a misspelling, or a type from a module this file does not import"
}

/// `"n=${n}"` -- string interpolation. The lexer keeps the literal whole, so
/// nothing is lost; compiling it means emitting a format call.
const INTERP_MSG = "outside the subset: only a bare name is compiled inside an interpolation yet (E2-b3) -- chains, calls and operators arrive with I2"

/// `Circle(r)` for `| Circle { radius int }`: a record-form variant destructured
/// by POSITION. D54 makes the brace form normative; the oracle's
/// E_RECORD_VARIANT_POSITIONAL_PATTERN (registry 719) is the same refusal.
const RECORD_VARIANT_POSITIONAL_MSG = "this variant is declared in RECORD form and is destructured by position -- write its fields by name, `Circle { radius }` (D54; the oracle's E_RECORD_VARIANT_POSITIONAL_PATTERN, registry 719)"

/// `import a.b.{X}` and `use a.b.c`. The parser reads both spellings; the
/// subset compiles ONE module, so there is nothing for an import to name yet.
const IMPORT_MSG = "outside the subset: `import`/`use` arrive with modules (E3)"

/// `export` in front of a declaration. Nearly every declaration in std wears
/// it, and until 2026-08-18 novac answered the most common form in the
/// language with "construct not in the MVP grammar" -- `export` was not even a
/// keyword, so the token fell into the top-level junk bucket.
const EXPORT_MSG = "outside the subset: `export` arrives with modules (E3)"

/// An `import` that RENAMES what it brings (`import a.b as c`, `import a.{X as Y}`).
///
/// Refused by name and not accepted silently, because section 9.1d point 2 is
/// IDENTITY: a C name is a function of the DECLARATION, never of the importer, so
/// an alias must change the spelling in the caller's source and nothing else.
/// Until cross-file resolution exists there is no declaration to point the alias
/// at, and accepting the word would leave a promise novac does not keep.
const IMPORT_ALIAS_MSG = "outside the subset: an `import ... as ...` alias arrives with cross-file resolution -- a C name is a function of the DECLARATION, not of the importer (274 section 9.1d point 2)"

/// `#realtime nogc` and its kin.
const ATTR_MSG = "outside the subset: an effect attribute is read but not compiled yet"

/// `unsafe { ... }` -- D216 s8. Compiled since 274.11 E.10 step 3c where an `if`
/// value stands (initializer, argument, a body's tail) and as a statement.
const UNSAFE_MSG = "outside the subset: an `unsafe` block is compiled as a statement, an initializer, a call argument or a body's tail -- not in this position yet (E2-b)"

/// An `unsafe` block with no operation that needs it -- a LANGUAGE error of the
/// oracle's too (D216 section 21 map, E_UNSAFE_UNUSED).
const UNSAFE_UNUSED_MSG = "this `unsafe` block contains no operation that requires unsafe context -- remove it (D216, E_UNSAFE_UNUSED)"

/// A raw-pointer operation outside `unsafe { }` (D216, E_UNSAFE_REQUIRED).
const UNSAFE_REQUIRED_MSG = "a raw-pointer operation requires an `unsafe { }` block (D216, E_UNSAFE_REQUIRED)"

/// A raw-pointer method novac does not compile yet: only `read_at` is.
/// A written type argument that names no type novac knows -- a call's and
/// `size_of[T]()`'s alike (one text, one refusal).
const TYPE_ARG_UNKNOWN_MSG = "outside the subset: a type argument names a type novac does not know (E2-b)"

/// A cast whose target names no type novac knows -- the named and the pointer target alike.
const CAST_UNKNOWN_TYPE_MSG = "outside the subset: a cast to a type novac does not know"

const PTR_METHOD_MSG = "outside the subset: of the raw-pointer methods only `read_at` and `write_at` are compiled yet (D216; E2-b)"

/// `write_at` through a `*T`: the language writes only through `*mut T` (D216).
const PTR_WRITE_RO_MSG = "`write_at` writes through a `*mut T` only -- this pointer is `*T` (D216)"

/// `test "name" { ... }`.
const TEST_MSG = "outside the subset: a `test` block arrives with the test runner"

/// A VALUE record -- `type Point value { x int, y int }`.
///
/// THE FORM IS READ (the parser learned it 2026-08-23, and six of novac's own
/// files stop here rather than on a syntax lie), and it is REFUSED because the
/// layout is what the word means. Measured the same hour, and this is why the
/// refusal exists instead of silence:
///
///     type Pt value { n int }
///     mut a = Pt { n: 1 }   ro b = a   a.n = 11
///     println(a.n)  println(b.n)
///
/// The fixed point prints `11` then `1` -- `b` is a COPY. novac, emitting the
/// record as a GC pointer like any other, printed `11` and `11`: the copy became
/// an alias. Accepting a form and miscompiling it is worse than naming it, and a
/// divergence in the PERMISSIVE direction is the worse kind of divergence.
///
/// What the layout wave owes: `NovaValue_<name>` by value (not `Nova_<name>*`),
/// `.` where a pointer takes `->`, construction on the stack instead of
/// `nova_alloc`, and the same for `@field` on a value receiver.

/// A method whose `value` record receiver crosses by POINTER (D488 R3/R5) needs a
/// PLACE to take the address of; a temporary receiver (`mk().m()`) has none yet.
const VALUE_RECV_TEMP_MSG = "outside the subset: this method takes its `value` record receiver by pointer (D488), and the receiver here is a temporary -- novac does not spill it to a place yet (E2-b3)"

/// A `mut` PARAMETER of a value record (the receiver's rule is D488, compiled
/// since 2026-10-01): the fixed point lowers a `mut x V` parameter to `V*` (plan 184,
/// P10 ABI), and novac passes a value record BY VALUE -- the callee writes to a
/// copy the caller never sees. Measured by the hunt (emit_c x K1, H4): the
/// oracle prints 2, novac printed 1 in silence. `ro` reads the copy legally and
/// `consume` owns what it got (a write is unobservable), so only `mut` refuses.
const VALUE_MUT_PARAM_MSG = "outside the subset: a `mut` parameter of a `value` record needs the V* lowering (the fixed point writes `NovaValue_X*`), and novac passes a value record by value -- the callee would write to a copy"

/// A VARIADIC parameter, `fn total(...args []int)`. The parser reads it (S9.4:
/// the grammar is the language's), and D69 -- it must be last -- is a position
/// rule the checker owns. Until 2026-08-18 the `...` broke the parameter list
/// apart and the refusal read "fn requires a declared return type", about a
/// signature that declares one.
const VARIADIC_MSG = "outside the subset: a variadic parameter `...args []T` arrives with generics (E2-b)"

/// A DEFAULT parameter value whose expression is not a NUMBER or a boolean.
///
/// The form itself is in the subset since wave 4; this is the one narrowing, and
/// it follows from what D102 says a default IS: an expression evaluated AT THE
/// CALL SITE, each call anew, allowed to mention preceding parameters and
/// module-level `const`. Emitting such an expression means writing it inside the
/// CALLER, where those names mean the caller's own bindings or nothing at all --
/// so what the subset takes is the values that mean the same in both places: a
/// numeric literal and a boolean. A STRING is refused with them, because a Nova
/// string literal may interpolate (`"${x}"`) and the interpolation would read
/// the caller's scope; nothing about the wave needs it (novac's own defaults are
/// `skip int = 0`, measured), and a refusal by name can be relaxed later.
const DEFAULT_EXPR_MSG = "outside the subset: a default parameter value is a number or a boolean here (D102 evaluates it at the call site, and any other form would be read in the caller's scope)"

/// D102 declaration-rule 1: defaulted parameters come AFTER the required ones.
/// Not a subset limit -- the language forbids it, and so does the oracle.
const DEFAULT_ORDER_MSG = "a parameter with a default value stands before one without (D102: defaulted parameters come last)"

/// A CAST, `1 as u8`. `as` was not a keyword of this lexer until 2026-08-18, so
/// the cast lexed as two names and the refusal read "unknown name" -- the
/// reader was sent looking for a binding that was never meant to exist.
///
/// Since the numeric wave (p274-carina-cast, 2026-10-01) the text names what is
/// still held, because the numeric family itself compiles: `as any`, a sum's
/// discriminant and an integer into a newtype. The pairs once held for a word
/// (`int as uint`, a char value into an integer, a bool into a number) were all
/// decided on 2026-10-01 (D54 amendment) and left the list.
const CAST_MSG = "outside the subset: this cast `expr as T` is not compiled yet -- the numeric family is; `as any`, a sum's discriminant and a value into a newtype wait (E2-b)"

/// THE FORBIDDEN PAIRS of D54 (spec/decisions/03-syntax.md): a permanent
/// language error, not a debt, so the head carries neither of the debt's
/// spellings. The pair and the replacement follow it (`cast_pair_msg`).
const CAST_PAIR_HEAD = "this cast is no conversion the language has:"

/// D54, the table of forbidden casts: a number is not a truth value.
const CAST_TO_BOOL_WHY = "a bool is not a number; write the comparison (`n != 0`, `f != 0.0`)"

/// D54 with its 2026-08-01 amendment: a string is parsed or printed by a
/// method on the SOURCE, never cast.
const CAST_STR_WHY = "a string is parsed or printed by a method on the source (`s.to_int()`, `n.to_str()`), never cast"

/// D54 with its 2026-10-01 amendment: a char VALUE goes only into a row that
/// holds every codepoint (i32/u32/int/uint/i64/u64); the checked narrow form
/// is `c.to_u8()`.
const CAST_CHAR_NARROW_WHY = "a char value goes only into an integer that holds every codepoint (i32, u32, int, uint, i64, u64); into a narrower one convert with a checked method (`c.to_u8()`, a Result), or cast a char LITERAL that fits"

/// D54 with its 2026-09-05 amendment: the one door into a char is `to_char()`.
const CAST_INT_CHAR_WHY = "not every integer is a codepoint; convert with `n.to_char()` (a Result), or cast an integer LITERAL, checked at compile time"

/// D54: `any` has no static conversion; its value is asked with `is`.
const CAST_FROM_ANY_WHY = "an `any` has no static conversion; test it with `is` (`x is int`)"

/// D54: "arbitrary types without an explicit rule -- a compile error".
const CAST_NO_RULE_WHY = "the language names no such conversion, and a cast without a rule is an error"

/// THE SHELL CARRIES NO SYMBOL for this method of this type -- ONE text for one
/// refusal. Until 2026-10-01 five sites spelled it five ways (`index` in
/// binds.nv, two in methods.nv, the slice's `index(Range)` and `end_index` in
/// slice.nv), with three different tails for one remedy: the interop surface
/// is the probe program, and the method arrives when the probe uses it.
fn no_shell_method_msg(method str, ty str) -> str =>
    "outside the subset: the shell novac links into carries no `${method}` for ${ty} -- the interop surface is the probe program (novac/probe/shell_probe.nv, E2-b); the method arrives when the probe uses it"

/// A range that is not the head of a `for`.
const RANGE_MSG = "outside the subset: a range is a `for` head here, not a value (E2-b)"

/// A SLICE, `src[lo..hi]` -- a range standing as the INDEX of an index
/// expression, which is a different form from a range used as a value.
///
/// Registry 1170, found 2026-09-20 by this window's own measurement: both
/// forms came through one door and the door answered for both with the
/// words of one, so a reader of `path[cut..]` was told about the head of a
/// `for` that his line does not contain. The class is the general one --
/// a shared path is POORER than the cases it absorbed -- and the repair is
/// not a second text beside the first but a question the door can ask:
/// WHERE does this range stand.
const SLICE_MSG = "outside the subset: a slice `x[lo..hi]` is read but not compiled yet (E2-b)"

/// A newtype over a number mixed with another newtype or with a value of its
/// representation (check/operators.nv) -- D52's amendment of 2026-09-04, rule 3:
/// a LANGUAGE error, not a subset limit, and the words name the way out.
const NEWTYPE_MIX_MSG = "the operands are different types -- a newtype does not mix with another newtype or with a value of its representation (D52): convert one side with `as`"

/// An index or a slice bound of a type other than `int` (check/binds.nv). One text
/// for both: measured against the oracle, it accepts a `u8` index and a `u8` slice
/// bound alike (2026-08-30 and 2026-09-25), so the refusal says SUBSET, not language.
const INT_INDEX_MSG = "outside the subset: only an `int` index is compiled yet -- the language allows other integer types here (E2-b, with the numeric family)"

/// The ONE text of the rule, so that the two doors cannot drift apart.
const FOR_HEAD_MSG = "outside the subset: the head of a `for` is a range `lo..hi` here; a collection head arrives with generics (E2-b)"

/// The must-consume declaration: read by the parser (the tree is lossless), refused here.
///
/// Carina has NO LINEARITY AT ALL -- the consumption obligation is checked nowhere
/// (registry #1099, measured 2026-09-14: `fn tag(consume c Cel)` with `c` never consumed
/// gives zero diagnostics). Accepting `type X consume` would therefore promise a guarantee
/// the compiler does not give, which is worse than refusing: a refusal the author sees, a
/// missing guarantee nobody does.
const CONSUME_TYPE_MSG = "outside the subset: `type X consume` declares a must-consume type, and novac checks no consumption obligation anywhere -- accepting the declaration would promise a guarantee it does not give; not compiled yet (274.7 B13)"

/// ONE CAUSE, TWO POSITIONS (E.9 step 2, 2026-09-30): an effect's runtime
/// pieces -- its operation table, its thread slot, its dispatchers -- are the
/// SHELL's, and the shell carries them only for the std effects its probe
/// uses. An effect a file DECLARES (`type X effect { ... }`, refused at the
/// declaration by @report_if_effect_type) and an operation of an effect the
/// shell does not carry (refused at the call, check/methods.nv) are the same
/// gap, so they say the same sentence. Until this wave the declaration's text
/// claimed novac typed no `with`, handler or operation call -- false since E4
/// and E.9. No generic parameter on the declaration -- D456 -- is a separate
/// question this message does not reach.
fn effect_without_runtime_msg(name str) -> str =>
    "outside the subset: effect `${name}` has no runtime pieces in the shell novac links into -- its operation table, thread slot and dispatchers are the shell's, carried for the std effects its probe uses; an effect a file declares is not emitted yet (E5, effect declarations)"

/// Type parameters in the wrong PLACE -- a different mistake from type
/// parameters not being compiled yet, and the message must not confuse them.
const GENERIC_POSITION_MSG = "type parameters before the name belong to a method receiver; a free function declares them after its name"

/// A BOUND on a type parameter (`[T Compare]`, `[K Equal + Hash]`).
const GENERIC_BOUND_MSG = "outside the subset: a bound on a type parameter dispatches through a protocol (D122), and that arrives with E2-b3 -- the parameter itself is compiled, the constraint is not"

/// A LINEARITY constraint on a type parameter (`[T consume]`).
const GENERIC_CONSUME_MSG = "outside the subset: a generic over a must-consume type (`[T consume]`) needs linearity, and novac tracks none yet (D133/D156) -- the parameter itself is compiled, the obligation is not"

/// One text for both doors (fn and type): the reader must not have to
/// wonder whether two wordings mean two different limits.
const GENERIC_MSG = "outside the E1 subset: generic parameters are not compiled yet (planned for E2-b2)"

/// A run the parser could not read at all.
///
/// Every FORM of the language is parsed since S9.4a, so this is no longer
/// a way of saying "the subset does not go that far" -- it says the text is
/// not Nova. Whatever the subset declines, it declines BY NAME somewhere
/// else, and `check-novac-no-grammar-excuse.py` keeps that true.
/// THE PARSER'S FALLBACK, and the one refusal that must NOT pick a side. It is reached
/// from both directions: the text really is malformed (a missing `)`), or the form is
/// perfectly legal Nova that novac does not read yet. The door cannot tell those apart --
/// that is WHY it is a fallback -- so the message says so instead of guessing.
///
/// It used to guess, and it guessed the worse way: "this is not a form of the language"
/// is a claim about the NORM, and a subset compiler is entitled to claims about itself
/// only. On a legal form it sent the author to fix correct code. Seven places in this
/// source already said in prose that the sentence lies (`lex/lex.nv`, `parse/decls.nv`,
/// `parse/expr.nv`, `parse/type_decl.nv` twice, `sem/slots.nv`, `check/check.nv`) plus
/// the fixture `fixtures/extern_decl/neg_1.nv` -- knowledge scattered across seven
/// readers and owned by none, which is what an absent home looks like.
///
/// The OPPOSITE lie was the trap in fixing it: "novac does not parse this form yet" reads
/// as a promise, and on a genuine typo it sends the author to wait for a wave instead of
/// closing a bracket.
///
/// NO STAGE, deliberately. A stage answers "when does this stop being debt", and this
/// refusal has no single answer: the bin is emptied span by span, a wave per form (plan
/// 274), and each form has its own date. One stage here would be a plausible number in
/// place of a considered one. It carries neither canonical debt spelling for the same
/// reason -- both of them promise "yet", and this refusal declines to promise.
const SYNTAX_MSG = "novac did not parse this -- and it cannot tell whether the form is one it does not read yet or the text is malformed; this is the parser's fallback, narrowed form by form"

/// A handler-literal op's `-> T` this door cannot resolve (check/handler.nv). The
/// undeclared name goes to the author's sentence first (`@report_if_ret_ref_undeclared`);
/// what is left is a type parameter of a generic body, not substituted there yet.
const HANDLER_RET_UNRESOLVED_MSG = "outside the subset: a handler-literal op's return type is not resolved here -- inside a generic body its type parameters are not substituted by this door yet (E4, handler-literal wave)"

/// `x is T` on a value whose type is known statically and is not a sum (D54).
const IS_NON_ANY_MSG = "`is` tests a value of type `any` or a sum -- the type of this value is known statically, so the test answers nothing (D54, E_IS_NON_ANY)"

/// `s is V` on a sum where V is not one of its variants.
const IS_NOT_VARIANT_MSG = "`is` on a sum names one of its variants -- this is not a variant of the tested sum (D54)"

/// `x is T` on an `any` where T has no fixed runtime type id.
const IS_TARGET_MSG = "outside the subset: `x is T` on an `any` tests a type with a fixed runtime id here (int, str, bool, f64, f32, u8) -- the id of any other type is generated per compilation unit (274.11 E9)"

/// A `spawn` outside a `supervised` body (D50; the oracle refuses it in codegen).
const SPAWN_OUTSIDE_SUPERVISED_MSG = "`spawn` is only allowed inside `supervised` (D50) -- wrap the code in `supervised { ... }` so the fiber has a scope that waits for it"

/// A `spawn` body capturing a `mut` binding of a type not vouched `#share`.
fn concurrent_mut_capture_msg(name str) -> str =>
    "a `spawn` body captures the `mut` binding `${name}`, whose type is not safe to mutate from two fibers -- a race; share it through a `#share` type (`AtomicInt`, `Mutex`, ...) or a channel (06-concurrency.md section 2, E_CONCURRENT_MUT_CAPTURE)"

/// A handler literal outside the two positions whose doors judge it (registry 1312).
/// `@type_handler_lit` is called by the `with` binding's handler slot and by a local
/// binding's initializer, and by nothing else: in a `=>` body, after `return`, as a
/// match arm, an argument, a block tail or an if branch no door typed the literal at
/// all -- silent on an unknown op, an ICE on a legal literal, or somebody else's cause.
/// The refusal is said by the walk gate, `@walk`'s HandlerLit arm (check/check.nv).
/// The spec calls those positions legal (`04-effects.md`, `Effect[E]` as a value); the
/// plan defers them (274.11 E.5), so the answer there is this name, not silence.
/// ONE BOUNDARY, TWO DOORS (E.9 step 3): a literal HELD by a local binding is a
/// handler value too, and one capturing a `mut` binding is refused by this same
/// name (`@type_bind`, check/binds.nv). A `mut` capture is taken by ADDRESS, the
/// `with` body ends inside the frame that address points into, a held value does
/// not have to (`ro h = ...` and then `h` returned); the box that keeps it alive
/// (D474 section 0, the closure scheme) is the handler-value wave's.
const HANDLER_LIT_POSITION_MSG = "outside the subset: a handler literal is compiled only as a `with` binding's handler or a local binding's initializer (`ro h = effect E { ... }`) that captures no `mut` binding -- a handler value in any other position, and a held one whose `mut` capture would be an address into its frame, is not compiled yet (E4, boundary E.5 of plan 274.11)"

/// `break` or `continue` with no loop around it (274.7 wave B10): a language error --
/// the jump has no target. A handler op's body counts as its own function.
const LOOP_CTL_OUTSIDE_MSG = "`break` and `continue` stand only inside the body of a `for` or `while` loop -- there is no loop around this one"

/// `==` / `!=` on a sum with no `Equal` in its `#impl(...)` (274.7 wave R): a LANGUAGE
/// error -- D363 routes equality through `Equal`, and a sum is a value type that opts in.
const SUM_EQ_NEEDS_EQUAL_MSG = "`==` on a sum needs the type to implement `Equal` (D363): a sum is a value type (D477) and opts in -- add `#impl(Equal)` above its declaration"

/// `..` in a tuple pattern before a position (D486 §2, registry 1382): a LANGUAGE error.
const REST_LAST_MSG = "`..` in a `(a, b, ..)` pattern stands only last (D486): a position written after it would count from the end, which that pattern does not have -- name the element by its position"

/// `..` in a variant payload beside positions (D486 §2, registry 1382): a LANGUAGE error.
const PAYLOAD_REST_MSG = "`..` in a variant payload stands alone, as in `Cons(..)` (D59, D486): a payload that names some positions lists them all"

/// A literal or `|` inside a condition's pattern (D486 §4 amendment 2026-09-30 p.1,
/// registry 1379): a LANGUAGE error -- it compares with a value.
const COND_VALUE_PATTERN_MSG = "a condition's pattern does not compare with a value -- a literal or `|` stands in it at no depth (D486): write `== Some(3)` or a guard"

/// A condition whose whole pattern is `_` (D486 §4, §7; registry 1381): a LANGUAGE error.
const COND_WILD_ROOT_MSG = "a condition whose whole pattern is `_` always matches -- it tests nothing (D486)"

/// A bare name as the whole condition pattern with no `ro`/`mut` (D486 §4 amendment
/// 2026-09-30 p.3; registry 1381): a LANGUAGE error, the oracle's E_AMBIGUOUS_IDENT_PATTERN.
const COND_BARE_NAME_MSG = "a bare name as the whole condition pattern needs `ro` or `mut` before it (D486, E_AMBIGUOUS_IDENT_PATTERN): `if n = f()` reads as a mistyped `==`"
